@page "/blogs/how-to-find-nuget-dependency-vulnerabilities-before-they-find-you-in-dotnet"
@using SharedModels
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(NugetDependencyAudit)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                A .NET project can reference a handful of packages directly while NuGet resolves many more
                <Highlight>transitive dependencies</Highlight> for its target frameworks. The resolved package graph
                is the inventory that dependency auditing examines; it is not a list of assemblies proven to load
                at runtime.
            </p>
            <p>
                <Highlight>NuGet dependency auditing</Highlight> combines inventory, known-advisory checks, and
                maintenance review. <Highlight>NuGet Audit</Highlight> runs during restore in supported SDK and
                NuGet versions. Its results depend on the resolved graph, configured audit scope, and advisory data
                available at that time.
            </p>
            <p>
                This article shows how to inspect direct and transitive package references, review known advisories,
                check deprecation and version status, and make configured audit findings visible in continuous
                integration.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <h4>Why Transitive Dependencies Need Review</h4>
            <p>
                A project file shows direct references, while <Highlight>dotnet package list --include-transitive</Highlight>
                reports the resolved package graph for the selected projects and target frameworks. Some resolved
                packages may not be loaded by a particular execution path, but they still deserve review because
                they can contribute code or assets to the application.
            </p>
            <p>
                A <strong>direct dependency</strong> is one your project references explicitly. A
                <strong>transitive dependency</strong> arrives through another package. Both affect restore and build
                results, and ownership of a transitive package can be less obvious, so include it in routine review.
            </p>
            <p>
                An advisory is a signal to investigate, not proof that a vulnerable code path is reachable or that
                an incident will occur. Check the affected version range, where the package is used, available
                remediations, and your application's context. Treat transitive findings with the same care as
                direct findings.
            </p>

            <h4>The Three Dimensions of Dependency Risk</h4>
            <p>Review these separate dependency signals:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Vulnerability advisories:</strong> NuGet Audit reports known advisories for packages
                    in the restored graph. Coverage depends on
                    the configured advisory sources and their data; an empty report does not establish that all
                    package risks are absent.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Deprecation:</strong> A package maintainer has marked the package as deprecated, often with a reason or alternative.
                    Deprecation is a maintenance signal, but does not by itself establish whether the package
                    receives security fixes.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Staleness:</strong> A newer version is available. That alone does not mean the current version is unsafe; review
                    release notes, support status, and compatibility before planning an update.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                NuGet CLI commands help review these separate signals. They do not replace advisory triage or
                compatibility testing.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>Step 1: Inventory Your Full Dependency Tree</h4>
            <p>
                Run this command from the project or solution root to inspect resolved direct and transitive
                package references for the selected target frameworks. These examples use the .NET 10 SDK
                command form; with .NET 9 and earlier, restore first and use
                <Highlight>dotnet list package</Highlight> followed by the same options:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
# List only direct dependencies
dotnet package list

# Include transitive references in the resolved restore graph
dotnet package list --include-transitive

# Output as JSON for tooling integration
dotnet package list --include-transitive --format json
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The output separates <Highlight>Top-level Package</Highlight> from
                <Highlight>Transitive Package</Highlight>. Review the <strong>Resolved</strong> version: it
                records NuGet's selection for that restore, which can differ from the requested range.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
Project 'OrderApi' has the following package references

   [net10.0]:
   Top-level Package:
                         Requested   Resolved
   &gt; Dapper              2.1.35      2.1.35
   &gt; FluentValidation    11.10.0     11.10.0
   &gt; Polly               8.5.2       8.5.2

   Transitive Package:
                                    Resolved
   &gt; Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces  9.0.4
   &gt; System.Threading.Tasks.Extensions 4.5.4
   ... (50+ more entries)
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Step 2: Find Vulnerable Packages With NuGet Audit</h4>
            <p>
                Supported NuGet versions run <Highlight>NuGet Audit</Highlight> during
                <Highlight>dotnet restore</Highlight> and report known advisories for the restored package graph.
                The default audit scope can vary with NuGet version and target framework, so configure it explicitly
                when you need transitive coverage. You can also query package findings. The
                <Highlight>--vulnerable</Highlight> command uses configured audit sources starting with
                .NET SDK 9.0.300; earlier versions use package sources:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
# Report vulnerable packages (direct only, by default)
dotnet package list --vulnerable

# Include transitive packages in the vulnerability scan
dotnet package list --vulnerable --include-transitive
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The output identifies each vulnerable package, its severity level, and a link to the advisory:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
Project 'OrderApi' has the following vulnerable packages

   [net10.0]:
   Top-level Package:
                         Requested   Resolved   Severity   Advisory URL
   &gt; SomeOldPackage       1.2.0       1.2.0      High       https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xxxx-yyyy-zzzz

   Transitive Package:
                                    Resolved   Severity   Advisory URL
   &gt; AnotherTransitivePkg  3.1.0      High       https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-aaaa-bbbb-cccc
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Severity labels help prioritize triage; they do not measure reachability in your application.
                Review the advisory and affected version range, then assess remediation for both direct and
                transitive packages.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 3: Configure NuGet Audit in Your Build</h4>
            <p>
                Audit warnings are produced during restore. Set the scope and severity threshold explicitly, then
                promote the relevant NuGet audit warning codes to errors if your policy requires CI to fail on
                findings. A root <Highlight>Directory.Build.props</Highlight> can apply these MSBuild properties
                across projects:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-xml">
&lt;!-- Directory.Build.props — place at your solution root, applies to all projects --&gt;
&lt;Project&gt;
  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;!-- Include direct and transitive packages regardless of SDK defaults --&gt;
    &lt;NuGetAuditMode&gt;all&lt;/NuGetAuditMode&gt;
    &lt;!-- Set the minimum severity according to your team's policy --&gt;
    &lt;NuGetAuditLevel&gt;high&lt;/NuGetAuditLevel&gt;
    &lt;!-- Fail on audit findings without changing unrelated warning policy --&gt;
    &lt;WarningsAsErrors&gt;$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1900;NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;NU1905&lt;/WarningsAsErrors&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
&lt;/Project&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>NuGetAuditMode</Highlight> set to <Highlight>all</Highlight> requests direct and
                transitive auditing. The <Highlight>WarningsAsErrors</Highlight> list promotes the audit
                diagnostics to errors without turning every compiler or analyzer warning into a failure.
                This example promotes <Highlight>NU1905</Highlight> if NuGet emits it. That diagnostic indicates
                a configured audit source does not provide vulnerability data; when no
                <Highlight>auditSources</Highlight> are configured, NuGet uses package sources and suppresses
                <Highlight>NU1905</Highlight>. The other listed diagnostics cover audit-data retrieval errors
                and advisory findings. With the <Highlight>high</Highlight> threshold, the policy blocks high
                and critical findings. Choose the severity threshold deliberately.
            </p>
            <p>
                You can override these solution-wide defaults per-project in the <Highlight>.csproj</Highlight> file, 
                which is useful when different projects in a solution have different risk tolerances:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-xml">
&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;!-- Enable or disable audit for this specific project --&gt;
  &lt;NuGetAudit&gt;true&lt;/NuGetAudit&gt;

  &lt;!-- Minimum severity that triggers a warning: low, moderate, high, critical --&gt;
  &lt;NuGetAuditLevel&gt;moderate&lt;/NuGetAuditLevel&gt;

  &lt;!-- Audit direct packages only, or all (includes transitive) --&gt;
  &lt;NuGetAuditMode&gt;all&lt;/NuGetAuditMode&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Audit runs when restore evaluates the graph. A later <Highlight>dotnet build --no-restore</Highlight>
                does not repeat that audit, so ensure CI runs restore with the intended configuration. A clean
                audit means no matching advisory was reported for that graph and data source; it is not a complete
                security assessment.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 4: Detect Deprecated Packages</h4>
            <p>
                NuGet package metadata can identify packages their authors marked as deprecated. Treat the result
                as a maintenance signal and review the stated reason and suggested alternative:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
# Direct deprecated packages
dotnet package list --deprecated

# Include transitive
dotnet package list --deprecated --include-transitive
            </CodeSnippet>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
Project 'OrderApi' has the following deprecated packages

   [net10.0]:
   Transitive Package:
                                Resolved   Reason(s)         Alternative
   &gt; OldAuthLibrary             2.0.1      Legacy, Critical  NewAuthLibrary
   &gt; UnmaintainedHelper         1.5.0      Legacy            (none suggested)
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The output can include a deprecation reason and a suggested alternative. Verify those details
                against the package's release notes and support policy before choosing a replacement.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 5: Identify Outdated Packages</h4>
            <p>
                <em>Outdated</em> means a newer version is available; it does not necessarily mean the current
                version is vulnerable or still supported. Use the list to plan updates and compare release notes:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
dotnet package list --outdated

# Include transitive packages that have newer versions available
dotnet package list --outdated --include-transitive
            </CodeSnippet>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
Project 'OrderApi' has the following updates available

   [net10.0]:
   Top-level Package:
                     Requested   Resolved   Latest
   &gt; Dapper           2.1.35      2.1.35     2.1.44
   &gt; FluentValidation 11.10.0     11.10.0    11.11.0

   Transitive Package:
                                    Resolved   Latest
   &gt; System.Text.Json               8.0.5      10.0.1
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The <Highlight>Latest</Highlight> column is a point-in-time comparison. For a transitive package,
                first look for an upstream update. An explicit reference can influence resolution, but check
                compatibility and dependency constraints before using it as a temporary override.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 6: Override a Vulnerable Transitive Dependency</h4>
            <p>
                If the upstream package has not yet released a fix, a direct reference may select a patched
                transitive version when the package APIs and dependency constraints remain compatible. Treat
                this as a temporary remediation and verify the resulting restore graph and tests:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-xml">
&lt;ItemGroup&gt;
  &lt;!-- Example only: use a verified, compatible patched version --&gt;
  &lt;PackageReference Include="Some.Transitive.Package" Version="4.2.1" /&gt;
&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                NuGet resolution and compatibility rules still apply; an override can introduce a downgrade
                warning or runtime incompatibility. Inspect the resolved graph, run regression tests, document
                the reason, and remove the override when an upstream fix is available.
            </p>

            <h4>Putting It All Together: A CI Health Check Script</h4>
            <p>
                Use inventory commands for review and let restore's configured audit diagnostics provide the
                CI gate. The commands below report separate signals; they do not by themselves guarantee a
                nonzero exit code when findings appear:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
dotnet restore
dotnet package list --vulnerable --include-transitive
dotnet package list --deprecated --include-transitive
dotnet package list --outdated --include-transitive
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Run these commands from a solution root in your CI environment. Do not parse human-readable
                output with a fixed grep pattern to decide whether findings exist; output formats can change.
                Configure NuGet audit diagnostics as errors for a reliable restore gate, and retain the lists
                as triage information.
            </p>

            <h4>Understanding the Vulnerability Pipeline</h4>
            <p>
                The audit result depends on the package graph and advisory data available when restore runs:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
Resolved package graph
        │
        ▼
NuGet Audit checks configured advisory data
        │
        ▼
Restore reports matching findings
        │
        ▼
Developer reviews scope, remediation, and regression tests
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Advisory publication and feed refresh are not instantaneous or guaranteed to meet a fixed
                interval. Run restore regularly and keep the SDK and package sources current, while recognizing
                that audits only identify known advisories represented in the data available to NuGet.
            </p>
        </How>

        <Summary>
            <p>
                Dependency auditing gives teams a repeatable view of resolved packages and known advisories.
                Keep these limits and actions in mind:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>dotnet package list --include-transitive</Highlight> reports resolved direct and
                    transitive references for selected projects and target frameworks; it does not prove which
                    assemblies load at runtime.
                </li>
                <li>
                    NuGet Audit is integrated into supported SDK/NuGet restore workflows. Confirm the SDK version,
                    advisory sources, and configured direct/transitive scope used by CI.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Vulnerability, deprecation, and outdated-package reports are different signals. Review each
                    with advisory details, maintenance status, compatibility, and project context.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Configure <Highlight>NuGetAuditLevel</Highlight>, <Highlight>NuGetAuditMode</Highlight>, and
                    audit warning handling in <Highlight>Directory.Build.props</Highlight> or project files.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A temporary direct reference can select a patched transitive version when compatible; verify
                    the resolved graph, test the application, and remove the override when upstream support lands.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Use audit findings to prioritize remediation, then verify the fix with restore and regression
                tests. A clean report is useful evidence about known advisories, not proof that a dependency
                graph is risk-free.
            </p>
            <p>
                For a remediation workflow that keeps package details out of domain code, see
                <NavLink href="blogs/swap-out-a-vulnerable-nuget-package-without-touching-your-domain-code-in-dotnet">Swap Out a Vulnerable NuGet Package Without Touching Your Domain Code in .NET</NavLink>.
            </p>
        </Summary>
    </ContentBody>
</Content>
